WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:30.940
[Sylvia Brodach] I come from a family of six children.
[Frances Goldstein] And I was the oldest.

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:34.780
[Sylvia] Yeah. In between us, there was our
brother, Chaim.

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:37.520
And after me, my mother had twins.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.040
But, ah, one of them died. Her name was
Freda.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.900
Then is Judith.

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:45.660
And a little brother, Yankov.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:47.800
[Frances] And we lived a very happy life.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:50.300
My father was a businessman.

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:53.860
And we were educated in Czechit [Czech] and in Yiddish.

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:56.740
We had the yeshiva in the town that we supported.

00:00:56.740 --> 00:01:01.640
[Sylvia] We never experienced any anti-Semitism
under the Czech government.

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:03.580
We associated.

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:05.940
My friends were Czech, you know?

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.600
So we had a very happy home life.

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:17.360
[Frances] The Hungarian army came in.
[Sylvia] And they occupied—

00:01:17.369 --> 00:01:18.520
[Frances] And they occupied the Karpath [Carpathians].

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:20.680
[Sylvia] They took our business away.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:23.920
[Frances] They just plain-blind came in and
then took over.  [Sylvia] They occupied it.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:26.260
[Frances] And they told you, "get out," and that was it.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:32.420
[Sylvia] There was, like, rumbling.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:35.940
Rows of tanks were coming through the town.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:38.900
And they parked those tanks in the center

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:40.940
and we heard those noises during the night.

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:43.500
And in the morning, there was a knock on our door.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:45.700
And there were two SS standing.

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:47.740
And he said, "Let's go."

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:59.240
[Sylvia] Towards the end, they started to
liquidate the ghetto.

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:01.500
They started to ship people out.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.600
[Frances] We went like they ship animals.

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:05.969
You know, those open trains?

00:02:05.969 --> 00:02:08.200
[Sylvia] They never opened up the cars.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:09.660
It never stopped.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:13.480
It just constantly was going.

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:16.540
And we were suffocated, practically, there.

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:18.360
And because there were so many people

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:20.080
crammed into a small place,

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:22.080
there was no water. People were fainting.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:24.360
Elderly people were dying.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:29.800
And finally, after three days of traveling,
the train suddenly stopped.

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:31.800
They suddenly opened up the doors.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:38.200
[Frances] And I heard they say that men here, 
this goes here. The people start panicking. 
[Sylvia] Everybody started coming—

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:42.120
[Frances] They didn't know, and they was going with
those rubber sticks, you know, here, there.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:43.940
And we were very scared. And we didn't know nothing.

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:45.360
I remember my father,

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:46.620
he was grabbing my brother, you know.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:48.300
And my mother, the four, the five—

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:51.260
the five kids. My mother and me, my sister,
we were holding together.

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:52.120
[SIGHS]

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:53.460
And as we come down,

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:55.980
they were... The SS pushed my father here.

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:57.820
[Sylvia] They announced it.

00:02:57.820 --> 00:02:59.780
[Frances] And we go here.

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:02.620
And my mother was going with me, with my sister.

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:04.360
And they was 8, 10, 11.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:05.540
They pushed her here.

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:07.540
And I remember, my, my mother—

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:09.400
the kids start crying, “Mommy, mommy,” you know?

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:13.760
And my mother pulled herself away from me. 
And she says, [speaks in Yiddish] 

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.680
“You should take care of your sister, 
like if you were a—” [Sylvia] I know, Frances.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:18.880
[Sylvia] The minute we stepped off the freight train,

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:21.100
we were separated, right there and then.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:24.380
My father and my older brother went on one side.

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:26.920
My sister, she was a tall girl.

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:28.860
So they made her go to one side.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:31.480
And I went with my mother

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:35.120
and with the, uh, younger children.

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:38.860
And my mother looked around.
And she says, “Well, where is, uh, your sister?”

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:40.760
I said, “They made her go over there.”

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:41.720
So she tells me,

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:43.060
"You run after her,

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:45.620
because she only has one dress on her."

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:47.180
And they pushed her in one side.

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:48.740
And I was running

00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:51.940
from one place to another and I don't know how.

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:55.520
Because in Auschwitz, you just did not 
run from one place to another.

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:57.820
But somehow, I caught up with her.

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:00.240
And, uh, I found her.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.780
And we were marching, you know?

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:08.000
They took us into a section, where
all the women arrivals came in.

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:13.200
And that was a big, long barrack. 
And you stayed there.

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:16.540
We were in Auschwitz for three days, in barracks.

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:23.400
And outside, all that going on, there was
a big band playing music constantly, day and night.

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:25.380
There was smoke, heavy smoke.

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:27.120
There were babies screaming.

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:28.680
You were looking where it's come from,

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:30.720
but you couldn't see where it's come from.

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:33.160
[Frances] Everybody was screaming and crying and running,

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:35.440
and they says, you know, "Don't cry,

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:40.660
you'll be after they go shower in there,
you'll meet them in afternoon to meet again."

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:43.740
[Frances] And since then I have never seen 
[Sylvia] That was the last time we saw

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:48.580
[Sylvia] my father, my brother, my grandmother, 
my mother, and the—and the four youngers.
[Frances] my mother, my father,

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:51.800
[Frances] and my sisters and my brother. 
That was the last time I seen them.

00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:02.180
[Sylvia] I had never heard anything about
my rest of the family.

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:04.960
Until one day, I was sitting by the window.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:08.660
I was mending the socks.
And this friend of mine walks into the room,

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:11.160
and he said to me, “Sylvia,

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:14.140
what are you going to give me? 
I have a surprise for you."

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:16.000
And I picked up my head,

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:18.000
and I saw my sister standing in the yard,

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:19.730
you know, in the courtyard.

00:05:19.730 --> 00:05:22.460
So first, I thought I was seeing things.

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:26.020
And then she gave a yell, 
and I ran out in the courtyard.

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:28.940
And we both fainted right there on the spot.

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:46.780
[Fred Margulies] I attended a, uh, Jewish
school—Adass Yisroel in Berlin,

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:47.880
as did my sister.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:52.780
Most of the time we were very aware of the fact 
that we live in dangerous times.

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:59.860
On Kol Nidre before we went to synagogue,
it's the custom for father to bless his children.

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:09.420
And I remember all the years that I can think back 
that when my father blessed my sister and me,

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:13.380
he always cried.

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:16.460
And I assumed that

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:19.380
it was

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:21.520
because they knew.

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:26.460
Small as you were, you—you realized that.

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:30.740
And, of course, we were confronted daily, daily,

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:32.520
with a swastika

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:35.800
and the hordes,

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:39.300
and, in the glass cases on the street corners,

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:42.520
the display of the Stürmer.

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:46.780
My father would sometimes pick one up 
in a train and bring it home.

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:50.600
So there was this feeling of

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:52.800
an impending doom.

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:02.080
He was taken away very early in the morning.

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:05.120
My mother was crying, and there was such to-do

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:09.140
that my sister and I woke up, and we went
to see what was going on.

00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:13.120
And, uh, I remember the policeman saying, 
as far as he knows,

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:17.700
that they're being deported to Poland.

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:23.720
And, um, that's the last time I saw my father.

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:32.880
When Kristallnacht came, it was a—a disaster.

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:38.020
And it became clear that life for Jews in Germany

00:07:38.020 --> 00:07:41.540
was... all over.

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:50.900
Decisions had to be made as to 
who should go and who should not go.

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:56.290
For example, one of my uncles and aunt 
decided not to send both of their children.

00:07:56.290 --> 00:07:58.420
They would send one child to test the waters

00:07:58.420 --> 00:08:00.760
and keep one child with them

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:04.120
to see if this would work, 
because there was no certainty.

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:06.700
It was actually fraught with danger.

00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:10.460
And we were made aware of the fact that we 
will be carrying a luggage tag on our lapel,

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:14.280
and all it would say is the name and 
the district in Berlin which we lived—

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:17.920
no street, nothing else, no identity.

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:21.080
And that we were to act as though 
we didn't know from where we came.

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:28.660
We left in the morning, early in the morning.

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:31.160
It was timed so that we would get to the border

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:34.080
at night, because we needed 
the cover of darkness, of course.

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:38.500
I remember leaving from Bahnhof Zoo,

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:41.600
and I remember that not—

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:45.260
about a minute or two out of Bahnhof Zoo,

00:08:45.260 --> 00:08:48.320
on the elevated tracks of the railroad,

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:51.720
came into view the hull

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:54.080
of this burned and destroyed

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:57.560
famous synagogue of Berlin, Fasanenstrasse.

00:08:57.560 --> 00:09:00.680
And that was the last

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:03.940
stab in the heart for a little Jewish boy,

00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:08.960
to see the burnt hulk of this famous synagogue.

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:16.020
I also remember very well that my mother took
me into a corner in this railroad compartment,

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:19.560
I remember well what she said to me,

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:22.880
in order to make sure I understood, because,

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:25.460
of course, I was a little boy very attached to my mother.

00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:27.440
It was a great love relationship.

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:33.020
In that little speech in the train,

00:09:33.020 --> 00:09:35.640
I always say I became bar mitzvah.

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:37.440
I have not been formally bar mitzvahed.

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:39.220
When I was 13, I was not bar mitzvahed.

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:42.080
I was bar mitzvahed when I was 11.

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:44.620
By my mother. No minyan, no rabbi.

00:09:44.620 --> 00:09:47.440
I was given my—my orders, my commandments

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:49.260
as to what to do.

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:51.160
And I remember that.

00:09:51.160 --> 00:09:54.560
I was to be the hero of the family—

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:57.760
tapfer zu sein—to be courageous.

00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:04.260
And so the journey took—took its last hour or two.

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:08.600
At the station, as we got off the train, uh,

00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:11.620
my mother said to me, you must not try—

00:10:11.620 --> 00:10:14.160
you must—must not turn around and look back,

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:15.860
and you must not cry.

00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:18.040
And she did not kiss me good luck—goodbye,

00:10:18.040 --> 00:10:19.660
because there was just no time.

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:22.300
We went to the left, we children.

00:10:22.300 --> 00:10:25.300
And I remember my mother and 
the other lady turned to the right.

00:10:25.300 --> 00:10:27.660
And that was the last we saw of them.

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:30.320
That was December the 6th, 1938,

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:31.560
in the middle of the night.

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:43.320
I remember having to go to a photographer

00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:45.640
in Hoogeveen to have pictures taken.

00:10:45.660 --> 00:10:49.040
I still have those pictures in 
the little Dutch folder at home.

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:51.640
And, uh, we got to England.

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:58.860
And that was, of course, the last I saw of
my sister, who unfortunately perished in Bergen-Belsen.

00:11:16.460 --> 00:11:19.040
[Anita Schorr] I was living, uh, with my family,

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:20.100
my father, mother,

00:11:20.100 --> 00:11:23.220
and a brother, who was five years younger.

00:11:23.660 --> 00:11:29.320
I went to school. We were living a life of wonderful

00:11:29.320 --> 00:11:31.420
affluence and happiness.

00:11:31.420 --> 00:11:33.580
We went skiing in the winter,

00:11:33.580 --> 00:11:35.760
swimming in the summer.

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:43.180
This is, uh, my brother and myself.

00:11:43.180 --> 00:11:46.120
My Jewishness was mo—mostly

00:11:46.120 --> 00:11:48.900
combined with—with sports.

00:11:48.900 --> 00:11:51.680
And we—we went Friday night,

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:53.420
sometimes, into the synagogue.

00:11:53.420 --> 00:11:55.960
But it was more of an Oneg Shabbat kind of thing.

00:11:55.960 --> 00:11:59.500
I know we drove with the car to the synagogue.

00:12:04.860 --> 00:12:08.080
You lost your childhood, I think, at that
moment.

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:10.540
You suddenly were wearing a star,

00:12:10.540 --> 00:12:13.240
and you were not allowed to be in a tram,

00:12:13.240 --> 00:12:14.600
sitting with other people.

00:12:14.600 --> 00:12:17.840
You had to be standing on—on one of the outside places.

00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:21.460
Very similar to discrimination in this country,

00:12:21.460 --> 00:12:24.240
where the Blacks were pushed to the side.

00:12:30.460 --> 00:12:32.380
In Theresienstadt, at one point,

00:12:32.380 --> 00:12:34.700
they were testing some, um,

00:12:34.700 --> 00:12:37.700
vaccine against scarlet fever.

00:12:37.700 --> 00:12:39.920
So people were sick.

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:43.680
Then they were trying to get 
some vaccine for jaundice.

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:46.040
I was laying in bed with my brother

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:48.900
that had an enormous jaundice.

00:12:48.900 --> 00:12:50.340
And my mother had

00:12:50.340 --> 00:12:53.540
very dangerous scarlet fever.

00:12:53.540 --> 00:12:56.580
She was burning up with enormous fever.

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:05.320
It was freezing. It was muddy.

00:13:05.320 --> 00:13:07.580
They didn't let people go to the
bathroom.

00:13:07.580 --> 00:13:11.200
And people had to relieve themselves

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:14.900
while they were standing with 
tears in their eyes.

00:13:14.900 --> 00:13:18.260
I remember one incident, my brother, uh,

00:13:18.260 --> 00:13:21.580
telling my mother that he was hungry.

00:13:21.580 --> 00:13:24.760
And she had nothing to give him. 
And she was crying. And—

00:13:24.760 --> 00:13:29.980
that was certainly enough for
me never to mention anything,

00:13:29.980 --> 00:13:31.440
anything at all.

00:13:41.960 --> 00:13:46.620
My mother said—now I was, 
at that point, almost 14—

00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:49.080
she said, “You are going to tell them you are 18.

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:52.560
And you volunteer. And you go, and you—

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:55.900
you go with the rest of the women.

00:13:55.900 --> 00:13:57.840
I will stay with my brother.”

00:13:57.840 --> 00:14:02.160
And my—her explanation was 

00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:03.960
that she has to stay with my brother,

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:06.380
because he would forget his name.

00:14:06.380 --> 00:14:09.780
And I must admit that

00:14:09.780 --> 00:14:12.380
I was very angry at my mother for

00:14:12.380 --> 00:14:15.200
a very, very long time,

00:14:15.200 --> 00:14:19.140
that she didn't save herself for me.

00:14:19.140 --> 00:14:22.080
I passed through the selection.

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:26.160
I was now selected to go to Germany to work.

00:14:26.160 --> 00:14:28.660
And I suddenly see that there was somebody—

00:14:28.660 --> 00:14:31.080
they were examining women.

00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:33.880
And somebody got up from the table.

00:14:33.880 --> 00:14:36.240
And she was bleeding.

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:38.140
And I lost it. I started to cry,

00:14:38.140 --> 00:14:39.700
“I want back to my mommy.”

00:14:39.700 --> 00:14:42.000
And they couldn't quiet me down.

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:46.520
And a—and a German lady, a guard, 
came over to me

00:14:46.520 --> 00:14:49.440
and asked me, “Why are you crying?”

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:52.100
And I spoke very nicely German.

00:14:52.100 --> 00:14:54.920
And I said, “I want to go back to my mommy.”

00:14:54.920 --> 00:14:57.500
She took me to her room,

00:14:57.500 --> 00:14:59.980
and she gave me cocoa,

00:14:59.980 --> 00:15:04.020
which I didn't see for years, 
and years, and years.

00:15:04.020 --> 00:15:05.740
And she says to me, “Now,

00:15:05.740 --> 00:15:07.740
I will tell you something.

00:15:07.740 --> 00:15:09.740
And please, listen to me.

00:15:09.740 --> 00:15:11.920
Don't cry,

00:15:11.920 --> 00:15:14.920
and don't try to get back to your parents.

00:15:14.920 --> 00:15:17.460
You are better off. You are here with women

00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:20.660
that know you. They speak the same language.

00:15:20.660 --> 00:15:23.100
And you're going out into Germany.

00:15:23.100 --> 00:15:26.380
You're gonna—you're getting out of here.”

00:15:26.380 --> 00:15:29.480
She really saved my life.

00:15:31.820 --> 00:15:34.640
And then I found out later

00:15:34.640 --> 00:15:37.080
that my mother, with my little brother,

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:40.280
they went to the gas chambers

00:15:40.280 --> 00:15:40.960
the next day.

00:15:40.960 --> 00:15:43.600
The moment we left...

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:45.540
The—the men left.

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:49.440
Then the women left for hard labor to go to Germany.

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:53.020
They took the older and the women with the children,

00:15:53.020 --> 00:15:54.240
and they gassed them.

00:16:18.800 --> 00:16:21.040
[Heda Kovaly] My father's name was Irving Bloch,

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:23.260
my mother was Marta,

00:16:23.260 --> 00:16:26.580
and I had a brother, Georg.

00:16:26.580 --> 00:16:28.820
Both my grandparents were farmers,

00:16:28.820 --> 00:16:31.360
which was quite unusual for a Jewish family.

00:16:31.360 --> 00:16:36.980
Both of my grandparents had farms 
and my parents grew up there.

00:16:36.980 --> 00:16:38.980
I remember my grandmother very well.

00:16:38.980 --> 00:16:44.240
She was gorgeous—white hair, very lovely.

00:16:44.240 --> 00:16:50.820
And she was, um, killed when she was 
86 years old by the Germans.

00:17:00.920 --> 00:17:02.780
There was, um, very little food,

00:17:02.780 --> 00:17:05.240
the work was dreadfully hard,

00:17:05.240 --> 00:17:07.840
and it was completely closed.

00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:21.860
It was so claustrophobic and there was 
absolutely not enough food.

00:17:21.860 --> 00:17:24.760
For me, Lodz was especially horrible

00:17:24.760 --> 00:17:26.980
because of the children.

00:17:26.980 --> 00:17:28.780
They cried, and cried,

00:17:28.780 --> 00:17:32.220
and then they stopped crying one day.

00:17:33.440 --> 00:17:35.140
They executed—

00:17:35.140 --> 00:17:36.720
I don't know how many people,

00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:40.940
because I never had the courage to go and look.

00:17:40.940 --> 00:17:44.440
Becau—they let them hang

00:17:44.440 --> 00:17:47.600
on the square for several days.

00:17:47.600 --> 00:17:49.500
And my mother went there,

00:17:49.500 --> 00:17:51.120
because she said someone

00:17:51.120 --> 00:17:53.520
has to pray for them.

00:17:54.340 --> 00:17:57.020
From time to time, the Germans came

00:17:57.020 --> 00:17:59.020
and they took people away.

00:18:13.480 --> 00:18:17.600
The Germans went from one place to the other.

00:18:17.600 --> 00:18:20.840
And then they let us go in the street.

00:18:20.840 --> 00:18:22.380
And there was this—

00:18:22.380 --> 00:18:25.600
always this officer—

00:18:25.600 --> 00:18:27.880
a German officer—

00:18:27.880 --> 00:18:29.860
who

00:18:29.860 --> 00:18:31.760
said this person go this way,

00:18:31.760 --> 00:18:33.800
and this person goes back.

00:18:33.800 --> 00:18:36.520
And they took all the

00:18:36.520 --> 00:18:37.580
old people,

00:18:37.580 --> 00:18:40.120
and the children, and took them away.

00:18:48.500 --> 00:18:51.380
We went out of the train.

00:18:51.380 --> 00:18:52.180
And they—

00:18:52.180 --> 00:18:55.300
right there, they started dividing women from men.

00:18:55.300 --> 00:18:57.600
And I only could see my father's face.

00:18:57.600 --> 00:19:00.600
And he said, “Take care of Mother.”

00:19:00.600 --> 00:19:01.620
And that was all.

00:19:01.620 --> 00:19:05.140
And I was—you know, I didn't see him walking away.

00:19:05.140 --> 00:19:07.680
It was just such a crowd, you see?

00:19:07.680 --> 00:19:09.600
And, um,

00:19:09.600 --> 00:19:13.520
we walked, hand-in-hand, with my mother.

00:19:13.520 --> 00:19:16.040
And there was Mengele.

00:19:16.040 --> 00:19:20.980
And he showed my mother the right side.

00:19:20.980 --> 00:19:22.740
And I wanted to go with her.

00:19:22.740 --> 00:19:24.260
And there was a soldier, and

00:19:24.260 --> 00:19:27.560
he threw me to the ground, and said, "You stay here.”

00:19:27.560 --> 00:19:29.800
“Where are these people going?

00:19:29.800 --> 00:19:31.500
What's going to happen to them?”

00:19:31.500 --> 00:19:32.920
And he said, “Don't be hysterical.

00:19:32.920 --> 00:19:35.460
And if you ask, you aren’t going to see them again.”

00:19:37.040 --> 00:19:39.960
Very vividly, I can—I can see

00:19:40.860 --> 00:19:42.500
pictures, like in a movie.

00:19:42.500 --> 00:19:45.040
You know, I can see details,

00:19:45.040 --> 00:19:47.560
but I have a—there is no connection.

00:19:47.560 --> 00:19:49.220
And the last, um,

00:19:51.500 --> 00:19:53.920
uh, night, um...

00:19:54.640 --> 00:19:56.420
I don't think

00:19:56.460 --> 00:19:59.160
I spoke to anybody the whole time 
I was in Auschwitz.

00:19:59.160 --> 00:20:01.640
I just lost all, uh,

00:20:01.640 --> 00:20:05.080
capability of communicating with people.

00:20:05.080 --> 00:20:08.320
And I sort of crawled aside.

00:20:08.320 --> 00:20:10.800
And I thought, my mother.

00:20:10.800 --> 00:20:15.860
I couldn't think about anything except
my mother.

00:20:15.860 --> 00:20:18.640
Moje máma.

00:20:18.640 --> 00:20:21.660
And I remember sitting on the ground

00:20:21.660 --> 00:20:23.660
and hold up my hand and

00:20:23.660 --> 00:20:26.880
there was this big, um—you know the dandelions?

00:20:26.880 --> 00:20:32.980
And it came to me and it landed on my hand. 
And I thought, my mother, she's here.

00:20:32.980 --> 00:20:35.900
I thought maybe she just died that moment.

